Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:07.909 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  so afghanistan is marking its 102nd  anniversary of independence on thursday  but it's also coming to terms with the  return to life under taliban rule in the  city of izadabad at least two people  were killed at an independence day rally  and in host taliban fighters were  enforcing a curfew to stop protests at  kabul airport children were hoisted  above crowds as people tried to flee the  uk says it's unable to evacuate children  who are unaccompanied president joe  biden says u.s forces could stay on in  afghanistan until all american citizens  are out  even if that means missing his august  the 31st deadline for troops to leave  we'll get more on that in a moment but  first let's go to charlotte bellows  who's live for us in a couple and so  charlotte we have this independence day  coming just uh days after the taliban  took control of the country and we've  seen these these pockets of protests and  violence  yeah there have there have been a few  incidents the biggest one happening in  assad about in kuna where  it's unclear really what has happened we  know two people have been killed and  eight people have been injured but it's  unclear how they were killed and injured  from residents accounts they say that  somebody actually stabbed a taliban  fighter and in the chaos the taliban  started shooting and then there was a  stampede and now we have two people  killed and eight injured there was also  a similar incident in jalalabad just  east of kabul where a crowd gathered and  two people were shot  by the taliban there and then in kabul  uh it's been relatively quiet actually i  mean i've been here during independence  days in the past and usually  the traffic is very heavy people have  the republic flag which is the green red  and black flag out of their cars  soldiers are waving the flags people are  singing there's loud music there's  really very little of that today there's  been isolated  crowds moving waving the flag and that  has been allowed by the taliban  there has been some isolated protests  where people have been chanting  the flag is my identity it follows  protests yesterday in in other parts of  afghanistan predominantly in the east  where people very upset that the taliban  has taken down the republic flag in many  places and raised instead their own flag  and so people are resisting that and  really pushing back uh and the taliban  have been allowing that for for the most  part uh in nangarhar for example the  governor there saying it's fine you  could you can carry on and use the flag  and celebrate like you normally would  all right so quietish around the city  what about the airport we've been seeing  chaotic scenes there  yeah that's been happening since  essentially since president ghani fled  on sunday afternoon people panicked and  have started flooding the airport then  these evacuation flights picked up  various countries sending in their  military planes and and thousands upon  thousands of afghans just making a run  for the airport trying to get on some of  these evacuation flights the taliban are  securing the perimeter at the moment and  the main problem is  in their crowd control technique which  is predominantly shoot in the air and  whip people to push them back and the  problem with that is is that many of  these people do actually have valid  documents and are entitled to get on  some of these evacuation flights but  they're having a hard time  getting past essentially this taliban  front line to get to their flights and a  lot of people just too scared really and  going home other people sleeping around  the airport so still a lot of chaos uh  anarchy as as people try to get on them  and the taliban try to push them back  the taliban  also admitting that since sunday 12  people have died in kabul  nato also confirming that number and i  spent some time in a hospital yesterday  and can confirm that that some of those  deaths did actually come from the  airport and its perimeter  all right charlotte we'll leave it there  for the moment uh that's the latest in  kabul charlotte villas reporting there  let's bring our guests now talent  massoud he's a strategic affairs analyst  and joins us from islamabad uh  so this timing of independence dave so  soon after the taliban takeover was  always likely to be something of a flash  point do you think it's it's something  that's likely to develop  well it could uh because uh you know  there are a lot of people in the cities  and especially in kabul and other places  uh where they are very disheartened  by the takeover of taliban and also that  they would never like  them to impose their rule on them so i  think they will find an opportunity to  sort of present and i think it could  take a turn if the taliban also try to  be very harsh on them so i think it's a  very difficult situation at government  in afghanistan because there are in the  cities of people who just resent the  rule  of the taliban but at the same time the  taliban have been able to impose their  will on them  because of the fact that they had a  rural support and also the military  completely collapsed the iran military  against them so i think at the moment  the ones will have to somehow the other  bear them and  the taliban leadership will also have to  modulate their behavior  in order to understand  the  present situation yeah what about this  this resistance movement in in jan shear  province we have ahmad massoud calling  for support he was writing in an opinion  piece of the washington post indeed do  you think he's likely to get that kind  of support that he needs  well i think that  you know he is trying to resist but i  doubt very much uh if that would be  successful and i think the taliban will  launch an attack on them  yeah but i think he's trying to show  that he's still one of those uh who have  been able to resist the taliban  supremacy  but it's only a matter of time then i  think the taliban will be able to  overwhelm it  because  it's very difficult to have this pocket  of resistance  in a place where practically the whole  of afghanistan is under their country  so how looking ahead perhaps to the  medium term how do you see it once these  evacuations are through at the airport  and that kind of settles down  might the international community  changed their tune on their at the  moment this apparent acceptance of  taliban rule  yeah i agree with you that there will be  a level of acceptance because there is  no other alternative  and the fact that the previous  government was also extremely corrupt  and inefficient and did not have the  support of the rural  areas at least and also within the  cities they had limited support of a  certain elite so i think considering all  that they think that there is no other  alternative but to sort of modulate or  improve the behavior  of the taliban leadership so that and  they are more and  you know sort of behave and conduct  themselves in the more civilized  that is the only thing i think and then  obviously they have the springs that  they could pull because  the  afghanistan is at the moment  it is facing a serious financial crisis  at the same time  you know it also is facing a diplomatic  price  so i think in considering all that  the taliban leadership is  will have to show some flexibility as  well so there will be flexibility on  both sides and i think there will be  some part of an arrangement and you  would see and i would agree with you  that over some uh over a few weeks you  will find that  there will be  uh better coexistence between the west  and how critical is it for the survival  of this or the success i should say of  taliban rule that they  are they include in their government  members of the previous government  well that would be helpful but i think  more than the previous government  because you know the members of the  previous government they're also very  corrupt and  they have a legacy here you know  following them so i i would say that it  would have been much better if they  would have chosen people who have a  reputation but have not it had not been  soiled  over a period of time  and and they should take much younger  people  in the cabinet rather than those old  people  who have uh served practically every  regime  and have brought a bad name to it  all right appreciate that thanks very  much indeed for speaking to us sir  thanks a lot thank you

